course: dictionary
lesson: 15550
title: Supersede: taking the place of something old
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To supersede something means to take its place because it is newer or better, so the old thing is no longer used. «Cars have superseded horses and carts for most journeys.» Horses and carts are still around, but people no longer use them for most trips.
explain: Supersede is a formal word, so you will meet it in books, science, history and rules. «The new rule supersedes the old one from Monday.» The new rule takes over, and the old rule stops applying.
explain: Look for the clues. Something new has arrived, and the old thing is set aside. If you can swap in the word replace and the sentence still makes sense, you are on the right track.
fact: Supersede means to take the place of something older.
fact: Supersede is a verb: supersede, supersedes, superseded, superseding.
fact: Supersede is a formal word, so you will often see it in writing rather than in chatty speech.
fact: Supersede is not the same as destroy: the old thing may still exist, but it is no longer used.
fact: «Supersede» can be swapped for «replace» in many sentences, such as «The new computer will supersede the old one.»
exercise: «The new computer will supersede the old one next week.» What does supersede mean here? | Break the old one into pieces | Keep the old one safe for ever | Sell the old one to a friend | *Take the place of the old one
exercise: «When a new rule supersedes an old one, what happens to the old rule?» | *It is no longer used | It is used more than ever | It becomes twice as long | It is painted gold
exercise: Which word could replace «supersede» in «Cars supersede horses and carts» without changing the meaning? | melt | sleep | *replace | shout
test: «The old map was useless once the new map superseded it.» What has happened to the old map? | *It is no longer used, because the new map has taken its place | It is still the best map to use | It was made bigger | It was given to the king
test: Which sentence uses supersede correctly? | Supersede is the name of a colour | A sunny day may supersede a loud dog | *A new theory may supersede an old one when new evidence appears | Lunch will supersede the bell
exam: In your own words, what does it mean when a new idea supersedes an old one? | It copies the old idea exactly | It makes the old idea more famous | *It takes the place of the old idea, which is no longer used | It is written in a different language
